description:
Rather than using treated drinking water for purposes other than drinking, this action is about using grey water or other, lower qualities of water wherever possible.

The fundamental idea behind this action is that we should only treat water for any given ourpose as much as the purpose requires it to be treated, and conversely, we shouldn’t use highly-treated water for a purpose that requiires less treatment. 

What follows from this is that we should not for example use drinking water to cool an industrial power plant, or for heat exchange to cool space inside a building. Neither of these uses requires drinking quality water. Instead, perhaps rainwater or grey water with a certain pH and salinity is sufficient.

To do otherwise is to waste in multiple ways: 
- It wastes high-value water on uses that require low-quality/ value water;
- It wastes the energy used to treat the water unnecessarily;
- It wastes system capacity and operational time and maintenance to send water through filters and treatment needlessly;
- It wastes the money required to pay for the energy required to treat and pump water needlessly;
- Whatever processes and fuels are used to power all of this handling, if they release greenhouse gases, then they needlessly consume the world’s carbon  budget, effectivly wasting it.

Instead, the use of water should be carefully controlled to ensure we only treat as much as we require and we don’t use water of a highernquality than required for any given purpose. Where rainwater can be used dire tly - e.g. on gardens- it should be used; where filtered water is required, it should only be filtered, etc. Similarly, if water is produced from one industrial process, it should be recycled on-site as much as possible and re-used repeatedly on industrial sites.

Water should be categorized based on its quality where the best quality is suitable for human consumption, whereas lower levels could include water that is:
- clean enough to wash clothes or dishes (for example) but not clean enough to drink or use in food;
- adequate for watering gardens and crops without introducing harmful bacteria into foods;
- suitable for flushing toilets or some showers;
- suitable for watering lawns and plants not to be consumed but in a landscaping setting where odors and other aesthetic qualities should be maintained;
- suitable for washing vehicles, buildings and other outdoor infrastructure;
- suitable for cooling indoor spaces, industrial towers and data centre computing equipment, where the water is used for heat exchange within a closed system.

These different levels of water quality can be achieved by:
a) acquiring the water from a variety of sources including traditional reservoirs, natural water bodies, and groundwater as well as rainwater, stormwater, greywater, and wastewater (from which recycled water can be produced); and then,
b) treating the water only as much as is required to produce the quality required for the desired end use; and finally
c) providing the different categories of water separate from each other through dedicated, clearly labelled infrastructure that delivers appropriate water quality to consumers and end uses.
